<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solovki prison camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Solovki special camp, SLON, Solovki special prison, Solovki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solovki prison camp was a Soviet place of detention on the Solovetsky Islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>, set up in 1923 in the Solovetsky Monastery, at first chiefly for socialist opponents of the Bolshevik regime. One of the first forced labour camps, known by the acronym SLON, it was the nucleus from which the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> grew: from no more than 3,000 prisoners in 1923 it held about 50,000 by 1930, many of them later sent to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal">White Sea–Baltic Canal</a>. As a special prison from 1936 it held many who were executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a>; all but five of the 1,116 prisoners shipped to the mainland in October 1937 were shot at Sandarmokh. It closed in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solovki-prison-camp.jpg" alt="Solovki prison camp" width="330" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:19240000-rail_connection_solovki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
